      "writtenOn": "2020-05-13T04:00:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This padding field here is heavily misleading, and can\u0027t be used, since it\u0027s not literally right after name[EXT4_NAME_LEN].   Perhaps it would be better to remove it, and then in the documentation of ext4_extended_dir_entry_2, document it to be located after the file name at the next 4 byte aligned boundary.\n\nI\u0027d also suggest consider renaming ext4_extended_dir_entry2 to something like struct ext4_dir_entry_hash since that\u0027s what it is; this should make the code much clearer.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2057,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2057,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "606d05233defbe35fcba5726eed1fbd8905a024e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1f5d74f4_86a5f850",
        "filename": "fs/ext4/ext4.h",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 2065,
      "author": {
        "id": 1038033
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-05-13T04:00:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm, you\u0027re locating the directory entry hash immediaetly after name[name_len]?    Thats going to be problematic on some archtectures where 32-bit integers have to be located on 32-bit aligned boundaries.   Given that the rec_len is rounded up to 4-byte boundary, it\u0027s not going to save any space in the directory.  It would be better to make the directory entry hash to also be at the next 4 byte boundary after de-\u003ename[].   Otherwise EXT4_DIRENT_HASH() and EXT4_DIRENT_MINOR_HASH() will cause CPU traps on some architecures with a 75% probability.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 2065,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 2065,
        "endChar": 46
      },
